1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,240
What's today's video about? Japanese life hacks and manners—things Japanese people just don’t do!

2
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:10,740
Scene 1: Japanese Coins & Wallet Weight – Why Your Wallet Feels Like a Brick

3
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:15,874
My trip to Japan was so much fun!

4
00:00:15,897 --> 00:00:18,548
That’s great to hear!

5
00:00:18,571 --> 00:00:24,805
But there’s something I *really* wish I’d known before coming to Japan.

6
00:00:24,828 --> 00:00:27,167
Like what?

7
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:29,925
Money stuff.

8
00:00:29,948 --> 00:00:31,882
Money?

9
00:00:31,905 --> 00:00:37,582
Yeah. Japan has *so* many coins.

10
00:00:37,605 --> 00:00:39,644
True.

11
00:00:39,668 --> 00:00:48,239
There’s 1-yen, 5-yen, 10-yen, 50-yen, 100-yen, and 500-yen coins.

12
00:00:48,262 --> 00:00:54,196
Right. In my country, we have way more bills.

13
00:00:54,219 --> 00:00:57,485
Like 1-dollar bills, for example.

14
00:00:57,508 --> 00:01:02,042
Exactly. So wallets don’t get heavy.

15
00:01:02,065 --> 00:01:06,065
Did your wallet get heavy in Japan?

16
00:01:06,087 --> 00:01:12,454
Oh yeah. It got *so* heavy.

17
00:01:12,477 --> 00:01:14,521
Why’s that?

18
00:01:14,544 --> 00:01:20,357
I bought drinks from vending machines every day.

19
00:01:20,381 --> 00:01:23,275
Vending machines are super convenient, though!

20
00:01:23,298 --> 00:01:29,865
They are! But I always paid with 1,000-yen bills.

21
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:32,587
So you got a lot of change back.

22
00:01:32,611 --> 00:01:38,777
Yep. I ended up with tons of 100-yen and 500-yen coins.

23
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:43,093
Coins really add up, huh?

24
00:01:43,117 --> 00:01:47,721
Totally. My wallet was bursting with coins!

25
00:01:47,744 --> 00:01:49,997
That must’ve been heavy.

26
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:54,240
It was! My pants even started sagging a little.

27
00:01:54,263 --> 00:01:57,330
That sounds rough!

28
00:01:57,353 --> 00:02:03,805
Yeah. I *really* wish I’d known this before coming to Japan.

29
00:02:03,828 --> 00:02:05,565
Known what?

30
00:02:05,588 --> 00:02:09,440
That I needed a small coin pouch.

31
00:02:09,464 --> 00:02:12,268
A wallet just for coins, you mean?

32
00:02:12,291 --> 00:02:18,188
Exactly! That way, your regular wallet stays light.

33
00:02:18,212 --> 00:02:20,454
That’s actually really smart!

34
00:02:20,478 --> 00:02:26,044
Yeah, and you can find cute ones at 100-yen shops too.

35
00:02:26,067 --> 00:02:30,243
True! There are so many fun designs.

36
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:34,667
I had no idea about this.

37
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:39,664
Knowing this before your trip would’ve helped you prepare better, huh?

38
00:02:39,687 --> 00:02:44,663
Definitely. This is a *must-know* hack!

39
00:02:44,687 --> 00:02:53,696
You can download today’s video script from the description and top comment!

40
00:02:53,696 --> 00:02:55,196
Scene 2: Japan’s Missing Trash Bins – Why You’ll Be Carrying Your Garbage All Day

41
00:02:55,196 --> 00:03:02,407
Besides money, is there anything else you wish you’d known before coming to Japan?

42
00:03:02,430 --> 00:03:04,120
Oh yeah.

43
00:03:04,144 --> 00:03:07,477
It’s about trash bins in the city.

44
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:09,594
Trash bins?

45
00:03:09,618 --> 00:03:11,585
Oh yeah.

46
00:03:11,608 --> 00:03:16,774
There are *almost no* trash bins on the streets in Japan.

47
00:03:16,797 --> 00:03:18,597
True.

48
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:21,787
Barely any, right?

49
00:03:21,810 --> 00:03:28,423
I was *so* shocked the first time I came to Japan.

50
00:03:28,446 --> 00:03:31,925
Did you need to throw something away?

51
00:03:31,949 --> 00:03:33,749
Oh yeah.

52
00:03:33,772 --> 00:03:38,373
I bought some delicious snacks and ate them outside.

53
00:03:38,396 --> 00:03:42,466
And then I wanted to throw away the trash.

54
00:03:42,489 --> 00:03:46,856
But there weren’t any trash cans, were there?

55
00:03:46,879 --> 00:03:48,578
Oh yeah.

56
00:03:48,602 --> 00:03:54,335
I spent ages looking for a trash can near stations and shops.

57
00:03:54,358 --> 00:03:57,125
That must’ve been tough.

58
00:03:57,148 --> 00:03:59,007
Oh yeah.

59
00:03:59,031 --> 00:04:02,210
Why aren’t there any trash cans?

60
00:04:02,234 --> 00:04:08,600
A long time ago, trash cans were removed from public spaces for safety reasons.

61
00:04:08,623 --> 00:04:10,957
For safety?

62
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:12,780
Oh yeah.

63
00:04:12,803 --> 00:04:15,680
There’s a special reason behind it.

64
00:04:15,703 --> 00:04:17,675
I see.

65
00:04:17,698 --> 00:04:22,531
So that’s why everyone takes their trash home.

66
00:04:22,554 --> 00:04:24,209
Oh yeah.

67
00:04:24,232 --> 00:04:25,643
Exactly.

68
00:04:25,667 --> 00:04:30,400
But I did see something that looked like a trash can.

69
00:04:30,423 --> 00:04:32,860
Where did you see it?

70
00:04:32,884 --> 00:04:36,684
Next to a vending machine.

71
00:04:36,707 --> 00:04:39,073
There was a box there.

72
00:04:39,096 --> 00:04:42,163
Ah, that one.

73
00:04:42,186 --> 00:04:45,986
But that’s not a trash can.

74
00:04:46,009 --> 00:04:47,776
Wait, what?

75
00:04:47,799 --> 00:04:49,819
Really?

76
00:04:49,842 --> 00:04:51,575
Oh yeah.

77
00:04:51,598 --> 00:04:54,484
That’s a recycling bin.

78
00:04:54,507 --> 00:04:56,841
Recycling?

79
00:04:56,864 --> 00:04:58,706
Oh yeah.

80
00:04:58,729 --> 00:05:02,162
Only cans and plastic bottles go in there.

81
00:05:02,185 --> 00:05:04,041
Got it.

82
00:05:04,065 --> 00:05:06,882
Can I throw away snack wrappers in there?

83
00:05:06,905 --> 00:05:08,475
Oh yeah.

84
00:05:08,499 --> 00:05:12,032
No, snack wrappers aren’t allowed.

85
00:05:12,055 --> 00:05:14,508
Understood.

86
00:05:14,531 --> 00:05:16,531
It’s all about manners, right?

87
00:05:16,554 --> 00:05:18,448
Oh yeah.

88
00:05:18,472 --> 00:05:22,722
Everyone in Japan follows that rule.

89
00:05:22,745 --> 00:05:28,243
But carrying trash around all day is annoying.

90
00:05:28,266 --> 00:05:31,233
It’ll get my bag dirty.

91
00:05:31,256 --> 00:05:32,893
Oh yeah.

92
00:05:32,917 --> 00:05:38,150
I wish I’d known this before coming to Japan.

93
00:05:38,173 --> 00:05:40,232
Like what?

94
00:05:40,256 --> 00:05:45,464
Just keep a small plastic bag in your bag.

95
00:05:45,487 --> 00:05:48,687
Like the ones you get when shopping?

96
00:05:48,710 --> 00:05:50,282
Oh yeah.

97
00:05:50,306 --> 00:05:53,715
Put your trash in that bag.

98
00:05:53,739 --> 00:05:56,472
Then tie it shut.

99
00:05:56,495 --> 00:06:00,098
That way, your bag won’t get dirty.

100
00:06:00,121 --> 00:06:01,759
Oh yeah.

101
00:06:01,782 --> 00:06:05,582
You can throw it away when you get back to your hotel.

102
00:06:05,605 --> 00:06:08,572
That’s a great idea!

103
00:06:08,595 --> 00:06:10,240
Oh yeah.

104
00:06:10,264 --> 00:06:14,197
This is a basic rule for traveling in Japan.

105
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:21,325
You can review today’s video with the link in the description and top comment. *Note: The site may be removed without notice.

106
00:06:21,325 --> 00:06:22,825
Scene 3: Struggles with Japanese Toilets

107
00:06:22,825 --> 00:06:26,836
After trash, let’s talk about toilets.

108
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:28,509
Toilets?

109
00:06:28,532 --> 00:06:32,783
Japanese toilets are so clean!

110
00:06:32,806 --> 00:06:36,497
They really are.

111
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:40,687
But I did run into a little problem.

112
00:06:40,710 --> 00:06:42,843
Like what?

113
00:06:42,866 --> 00:06:47,939
After washing my hands, there was no paper to dry them with.

114
00:06:47,963 --> 00:06:51,211
Oh, you mean paper towels?

115
00:06:51,235 --> 00:06:57,413
Yeah. In my country, there are always paper towels.

116
00:06:57,436 --> 00:06:59,770
Got it.

117
00:06:59,793 --> 00:07:03,870
Japanese toilets don’t usually have them.

118
00:07:03,893 --> 00:07:08,665
The hand dryers weren’t working either.

119
00:07:08,689 --> 00:07:14,622
Yes. Since it's a health precaution, we can't use it right now.

120
00:07:14,645 --> 00:07:19,249
So, I wiped my wet hands on my pants.

121
00:07:19,273 --> 00:07:23,040
Heh, that must have been a bit awkward.

122
00:07:23,063 --> 00:07:27,496
Right. What do people in Japan usually do?

123
00:07:27,519 --> 00:07:32,154
Everyone carries their own handkerchief or towel.

124
00:07:32,178 --> 00:07:35,986
Do men carry handkerchiefs too?

125
00:07:36,010 --> 00:07:40,810
Yes, of course men carry them as well.

126
00:07:40,833 --> 00:07:42,866
Got it.

127
00:07:42,889 --> 00:07:47,547
I wish I had known that before coming to Japan.

128
00:07:47,571 --> 00:07:52,104
Make sure to keep a small handkerchief in your bag every day.

129
00:07:52,127 --> 00:07:58,860
Got it. I bought a bunch of cute mini towels in Japan.

130
00:07:58,883 --> 00:08:00,952
That’s nice!

131
00:08:00,976 --> 00:08:04,421
They also make great souvenirs.

132
00:08:04,445 --> 00:08:09,022
Yeah, my friends loved them too.

133
00:08:09,045 --> 00:08:13,245
Did you run into any other issues in the restroom?

134
00:08:13,268 --> 00:08:19,538
Yes. In an old park restroom, there was no soap.

135
00:08:19,562 --> 00:08:24,045
Ah, some restrooms don’t have soap, do they?

136
00:08:24,069 --> 00:08:28,310
I couldn’t wash my hands, so it was really inconvenient.

137
00:08:28,333 --> 00:08:33,206
Portable soap would’ve come in handy then.

138
00:08:33,229 --> 00:08:36,031
Portable soap?

139
00:08:36,054 --> 00:08:42,188
Yes. There’s paper soap and liquid soap in small sizes.

140
00:08:42,211 --> 00:08:44,983
Where can I buy those?

141
00:08:45,006 --> 00:08:49,822
You can find them at 100-yen shops or drugstores.

142
00:08:49,846 --> 00:08:54,413
100-yen shops? That’s so affordable!

143
00:08:54,436 --> 00:09:00,369
Yeah, they’re small, so they won’t weigh down your bag.

144
00:09:00,392 --> 00:09:02,327
That’s nice!

145
00:09:02,350 --> 00:09:06,444
I wish I had known about this before coming to Japan too.

146
00:09:06,468 --> 00:09:11,315
Right? Being prepared makes things so much easier.

147
00:09:11,338 --> 00:09:12,838
Scene 4: Street Food Etiquette

148
00:09:12,838 --> 00:09:18,275
Next up: food etiquette. Etiquette?

149
00:09:18,298 --> 00:09:21,075
What kind of rules are there?

150
00:09:21,099 --> 00:09:25,379
Eating while walking.

151
00:09:25,402 --> 00:09:29,441
Ah, eating on the go.

152
00:09:29,464 --> 00:09:31,237
Oh yeah.

153
00:09:31,261 --> 00:09:36,395
It’s normal in my country, but is it not allowed in Japan?

154
00:09:36,418 --> 00:09:38,253
Oh yeah.

155
00:09:38,276 --> 00:09:42,710
In Japan, eating while walking is considered bad manners.

156
00:09:42,733 --> 00:09:46,933
Why isn’t it allowed?

157
00:09:46,956 --> 00:09:52,834
You might spill food on someone’s clothes, which is rude.

158
00:09:52,857 --> 00:09:54,591
On their clothes?

159
00:09:54,614 --> 00:09:59,380
Definitely—imagine getting ketchup on your outfit!

160
00:09:59,403 --> 00:10:01,097
Oh yeah.

161
00:10:01,121 --> 00:10:05,754
So, you eat near where you bought the food.

162
00:10:05,777 --> 00:10:08,311
Right by the shop, you mean?

163
00:10:08,334 --> 00:10:10,340
Exactly.

164
00:10:10,363 --> 00:10:14,101
Finish eating there before you start walking again.

165
00:10:14,125 --> 00:10:16,058
Understood.

166
00:10:16,081 --> 00:10:19,118
Is it okay to eat next to a convenience store?

167
00:10:19,141 --> 00:10:20,675
Oh yeah.

168
00:10:20,698 --> 00:10:25,498
Yes, eat by the convenience store or at a nearby bench.

169
00:10:25,521 --> 00:10:27,979
What about ice cream?

170
00:10:28,002 --> 00:10:32,619
Eat your ice cream in front of the shop too.

171
00:10:32,642 --> 00:10:34,455
Understood.

172
00:10:34,479 --> 00:10:36,812
Eat it right there on the spot.

173
00:10:36,835 --> 00:10:40,802
Train etiquette is also important.

174
00:10:40,826 --> 00:10:42,959
Trains, huh?

175
00:10:42,982 --> 00:10:47,382
Japanese trains are so quiet.

176
00:10:47,405 --> 00:10:49,206
True.

177
00:10:49,229 --> 00:10:52,431
Everyone is so quiet, isn’t it?

178
00:10:52,455 --> 00:10:57,327
I wanted to make a phone call on the train.

179
00:10:57,351 --> 00:11:00,284
Wait, did you make a call?

180
00:11:00,307 --> 00:11:06,401
No. Since no one was talking, I didn’t call.

181
00:11:06,424 --> 00:11:08,558
That’s good.

182
00:11:08,581 --> 00:11:12,028
You shouldn’t make calls on the train.

183
00:11:12,051 --> 00:11:15,018
What about cell phones?

184
00:11:15,041 --> 00:11:18,060
Put it on silent mode.

185
00:11:18,084 --> 00:11:20,503
Manner mode, right?

186
00:11:20,526 --> 00:11:23,023
Yes, that’s right.

187
00:11:23,047 --> 00:11:26,489
Is it also not okay to listen to music through the speaker?

188
00:11:26,513 --> 00:11:29,446
No, that’s not allowed.

189
00:11:29,469 --> 00:11:32,202
Please use headphones.

190
00:11:32,225 --> 00:11:37,592
It’s completely different from my country, so I was surprised at first.

191
00:11:37,615 --> 00:11:41,651
But don’t you like how quiet the trains are?

192
00:11:41,674 --> 00:11:45,132
Yes, I really like it.

193
00:11:45,155 --> 00:11:49,048
It’s so quiet that I can sleep well.

194
00:11:49,071 --> 00:11:50,671
Hehe, yeah.

195
00:11:50,694 --> 00:11:53,961
A lot of people seem to be sleeping soundly.

196
00:11:53,984 --> 00:11:55,584
Oh yeah.

197
00:11:55,607 --> 00:12:00,240
Japanese trains are very safe, so you can relax.

198
00:12:00,263 --> 00:12:03,673
Yes, that’s really true.

199
00:12:03,697 --> 00:12:08,297
Knowing the manners makes traveling even more enjoyable.

200
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:09,899
True.

201
00:12:09,922 --> 00:12:14,219
Follow the manners and have a great trip!

202
00:12:14,242 --> 00:12:15,742
Scene 5: Troubles with Internet and Wi-Fi While Traveling

203
00:12:15,742 --> 00:12:20,309
Next up, let’s talk about the internet.

204
00:12:20,332 --> 00:12:22,665
The internet?

205
00:12:22,688 --> 00:12:28,124
Yes, I had a really hard time with it while traveling.

206
00:12:28,147 --> 00:12:30,681
Why’s that?

207
00:12:30,704 --> 00:12:35,002
I arrived at a Japanese airport.

208
00:12:35,025 --> 00:12:39,993
After that, I wanted to go straight to my hotel.

209
00:12:40,016 --> 00:12:42,249
Right, makes sense.

210
00:12:42,272 --> 00:12:46,506
But I didn’t know how to transfer trains.

211
00:12:46,529 --> 00:12:51,095
Didn’t you look it up on your smartphone?

212
00:12:51,118 --> 00:12:55,392
I wanted to use the free Wi-Fi at the airport.

213
00:12:55,416 --> 00:12:59,482
But it wouldn’t connect at all.

214
00:12:59,505 --> 00:13:06,239
Ah, airport Wi-Fi can be spotty sometimes.

215
00:13:06,262 --> 00:13:11,201
Yeah, so I couldn’t figure out the train route.

216
00:13:11,225 --> 00:13:15,025
That must’ve been really frustrating.

217
00:13:15,047 --> 00:13:18,781
Yes, I was so anxious.

218
00:13:18,804 --> 00:13:24,837
You should prepare your internet access before coming to Japan.

219
00:13:24,860 --> 00:13:26,956
Before coming?

220
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:33,580
Yes, set up an eSIM before boarding your flight.

221
00:13:33,603 --> 00:13:35,897
An eSIM?

222
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:42,854
Yes, you can start using it right away through your phone’s settings.

223
00:13:42,877 --> 00:13:45,243
That’s convenient!

224
00:13:45,266 --> 00:13:50,400
Yeah, set it up before arriving in Japan.

225
00:13:50,423 --> 00:13:56,823
That way, you can use the internet right away at the airport.

226
00:13:56,846 --> 00:14:02,379
I see. I didn’t know that.

227
00:14:02,402 --> 00:14:05,820
There’s also pocket Wi-Fi.

228
00:14:05,844 --> 00:14:08,377
Pocket Wi-Fi?

229
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:13,139
Yes, reserve it online beforehand.

230
00:14:13,162 --> 00:14:17,058
Then pick it up at the airport in Japan.

231
00:14:17,082 --> 00:14:20,849
You can pick it up at the airport?

232
00:14:20,872 --> 00:14:24,405
Yes, it's really easy.

233
00:14:24,428 --> 00:14:26,795
That’s nice!

234
00:14:26,818 --> 00:14:31,985
I was planning to rent Wi-Fi at the hotel.

235
00:14:32,008 --> 00:14:36,674
But I need internet even before I get to the hotel.

236
00:14:36,697 --> 00:14:43,631
Yes, I really need it because I don’t know my way around.

237
00:14:43,654 --> 00:14:49,620
So make sure to prepare before you come to Japan.

238
00:14:49,643 --> 00:14:55,577
Got it! For my next trip, I’ll set up an eSIM in advance.

239
00:14:55,600 --> 00:15:00,734
Good idea. It’s so much more reassuring.

240
00:15:00,757 --> 00:15:04,157
Yes, thank you!

241
00:15:04,180 --> 00:15:05,680
Scene 6: Travel Luggage and Trains

242
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:10,646
Next up—luggage.

243
00:15:10,669 --> 00:15:12,505
Luggage?

244
00:15:12,529 --> 00:15:16,329
Do you travel with a lot of stuff?

245
00:15:16,352 --> 00:15:20,152
Yeah, way too much.

246
00:15:20,175 --> 00:15:23,653
I had a huge suitcase.

247
00:15:23,676 --> 00:15:25,353
True.

248
00:15:25,376 --> 00:15:29,943
Travel luggage gets heavy, doesn’t it?

249
00:15:29,966 --> 00:15:36,599
Yeah, I took the train with my heavy suitcase.

250
00:15:36,622 --> 00:15:40,222
That must’ve been tough.

251
00:15:40,245 --> 00:15:47,379
It was exhausting—there were so many stairs!

252
00:15:47,402 --> 00:15:52,090
Older stations in Japan have a lot of stairs, don’t they?

253
00:15:52,113 --> 00:15:57,113
Yeah, even finding an elevator was a hassle.

254
00:15:57,136 --> 00:16:03,515
Carrying big luggage on a packed train is such a pain.

255
00:16:03,539 --> 00:16:09,872
Yeah, I was so nervous about getting in people’s way.

256
00:16:09,895 --> 00:16:11,539
True.

257
00:16:11,562 --> 00:16:14,696
It’s tough for everyone.

258
00:16:14,719 --> 00:16:19,378
I wish I’d known this before coming to Japan.

259
00:16:19,401 --> 00:16:21,934
What kind of hack?

260
00:16:21,957 --> 00:16:25,524
There’s a luggage delivery service.

261
00:16:25,547 --> 00:16:28,714
Oh, like takkyubin?

262
00:16:28,737 --> 00:16:36,408
Yes! They’ll send your luggage from the airport straight to your next hotel.

263
00:16:36,431 --> 00:16:38,248
True.

264
00:16:38,272 --> 00:16:41,472
It’s such a convenient service.

265
00:16:41,495 --> 00:16:46,763
I had my luggage sent from the hotel front desk.

266
00:16:46,787 --> 00:16:51,156
Did the hotel staff help you with it?

267
00:16:51,179 --> 00:16:55,716
Yes, the staff was really kind and assisted me.

268
00:16:55,739 --> 00:16:58,342
That’s great to hear!

269
00:16:58,366 --> 00:17:03,238
My luggage arrived at the hotel the next day.

270
00:17:03,262 --> 00:17:05,862
How much did it cost?

271
00:17:05,885 --> 00:17:10,618
Around 2,000 yen for one suitcase.

272
00:17:10,641 --> 00:17:12,841
That’s cheap!

273
00:17:12,864 --> 00:17:16,723
Yeah, it’s super affordable.

274
00:17:16,747 --> 00:17:22,438
Traveling became so much easier without heavy luggage.

275
00:17:22,462 --> 00:17:26,862
No physical strain—it’s great!

276
00:17:26,885 --> 00:17:30,937
Yeah, it was a lifesaver.

277
00:17:30,960 --> 00:17:36,037
I’ll definitely use this service again on my next trip.

278
00:17:36,061 --> 00:17:38,194
You should!

279
00:17:38,217 --> 00:17:41,550
Some staff even offer to help send your luggage, like, 'Shall we ship this for you?'

280
00:17:41,573 --> 00:17:45,464
There are so many helpful staff members.

281
00:17:45,487 --> 00:17:49,966
Yes, it’s such a thoughtful service.

282
00:17:49,990 --> 00:17:54,399
Absolutely—it’s one of the wonderful things about Japan.

283
00:17:54,423 --> 00:17:55,923
Scene 7: Coping with Fatigue from Walking During Travel

284
00:17:55,923 --> 00:18:01,890
After luggage, let’s talk about your feet. Your feet?

285
00:18:01,913 --> 00:18:04,758
Did something happen?

286
00:18:04,781 --> 00:18:06,149
Oh yeah.

287
00:18:06,173 --> 00:18:11,829
Traveling in Japan means walking *a lot*.

288
00:18:11,852 --> 00:18:13,630
True.

289
00:18:13,654 --> 00:18:17,588
How much did you walk each day?

290
00:18:17,611 --> 00:18:21,681
I walked about 20,000 steps every day.

291
00:18:21,704 --> 00:18:24,070
20,000 steps?

292
00:18:24,093 --> 00:18:28,333
That’s a *lot* of walking!

293
00:18:28,357 --> 00:18:29,724
Oh yeah.

294
00:18:29,747 --> 00:18:34,313
No wonder my feet were killing me.

295
00:18:34,336 --> 00:18:38,303
Foot pain is the worst, isn’t it?

296
00:18:38,326 --> 00:18:39,926
Oh yeah.

297
00:18:39,949 --> 00:18:44,785
At night, I could barely move in my hotel room.

298
00:18:44,808 --> 00:18:46,783
Got it.

299
00:18:46,807 --> 00:18:50,545
Did you do anything to help with that?

300
00:18:50,568 --> 00:18:54,302
No, I had no idea what to do.

301
00:18:54,325 --> 00:18:59,763
I wish I’d known this before coming to Japan.

302
00:18:59,786 --> 00:19:02,353
What’s a good hack for this?

303
00:19:02,376 --> 00:19:07,213
Grab those blue cooling sheets from the drugstore.

304
00:19:07,236 --> 00:19:10,931
Oh, the ones you stick on your feet!

305
00:19:10,955 --> 00:19:12,398
Oh yeah.

306
00:19:12,421 --> 00:19:15,880
Put them on before bed.

307
00:19:15,904 --> 00:19:17,304
How’d it work?

308
00:19:17,327 --> 00:19:22,193
The next morning, my feet felt *so* much lighter!

309
00:19:22,216 --> 00:19:25,149
I was shocked!

310
00:19:25,172 --> 00:19:28,147
That’s amazing!

311
00:19:28,171 --> 00:19:30,846
What are they called?

312
00:19:30,870 --> 00:19:33,837
Kyusoku Jikan.

313
00:19:33,860 --> 00:19:38,664
They’re super popular with foreign travelers!

314
00:19:38,687 --> 00:19:41,620
Kyusoku Jikan?

315
00:19:41,644 --> 00:19:44,513
That’s a fun name!

316
00:19:44,536 --> 00:19:45,746
Oh yeah.

317
00:19:45,770 --> 00:19:51,581
You can find them cheap at drugstores or Don Quijote.

318
00:19:51,605 --> 00:19:53,372
Got it.

319
00:19:53,395 --> 00:19:56,561
I’ll have to try them!

320
00:19:56,584 --> 00:19:58,158
Oh yeah.

321
00:19:58,182 --> 00:20:00,632
I *highly* recommend them.

322
00:20:00,655 --> 00:20:04,655
Did you do anything else for your feet?

323
00:20:04,678 --> 00:20:07,678
I took baths at the hotel.

324
00:20:07,701 --> 00:20:09,718
Baths?

325
00:20:09,741 --> 00:20:11,307
Oh yeah.

326
00:20:11,330 --> 00:20:16,580
Every night, I soaked in warm water for about five minutes.

327
00:20:16,604 --> 00:20:19,138
Just five minutes?

328
00:20:19,161 --> 00:20:20,538
Oh yeah.

329
00:20:20,561 --> 00:20:26,000
Yep—just five minutes, but my feet felt totally different the next day!

330
00:20:26,024 --> 00:20:27,824
Got it.

331
00:20:27,847 --> 00:20:31,780
Hotels with big baths are the best.

332
00:20:31,803 --> 00:20:33,370
Oh yeah.

333
00:20:33,393 --> 00:20:37,831
A big bath is *so* relaxing.

334
00:20:37,854 --> 00:20:39,459
Wow.

335
00:20:39,483 --> 00:20:42,159
It really melts the fatigue away.

336
00:20:42,183 --> 00:20:43,583
Oh yeah.

337
00:20:43,606 --> 00:20:48,606
So, picking a hotel with a big bath is a great tip!

338
00:20:48,629 --> 00:20:51,562
That’s a fantastic hack!

339
00:20:51,585 --> 00:20:54,952
I’ll definitely do that on my next trip.

340
00:20:54,975 --> 00:20:56,418
Oh yeah.

341
00:20:56,441 --> 00:20:58,884
You should!

342
00:20:58,907 --> 00:21:00,407
Scene 8: Smartphone Hacks / Convenient Ways to Use Your Smartphone

343
00:21:00,407 --> 00:21:07,176
Next up: smartphones. You used yours a lot, right?

344
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:10,567
Did you use it every day?

345
00:21:10,590 --> 00:21:11,990
Oh yeah.

346
00:21:12,013 --> 00:21:16,013
I used the maps app all the time.

347
00:21:16,035 --> 00:21:19,169
Maps are *so* handy.

348
00:21:19,192 --> 00:21:21,031
Oh yeah.

349
00:21:21,054 --> 00:21:24,924
I also checked train transfers daily.

350
00:21:24,947 --> 00:21:26,718
True.

351
00:21:26,742 --> 00:21:30,708
Japanese trains can be complicated!

352
00:21:30,731 --> 00:21:32,345
Oh yeah.

353
00:21:32,368 --> 00:21:35,261
I took tons of photos too.

354
00:21:35,285 --> 00:21:38,818
And I used a translation app.

355
00:21:38,841 --> 00:21:41,575
You used it *a lot*, huh?

356
00:21:41,598 --> 00:21:43,131
Oh yeah.

357
00:21:43,154 --> 00:21:49,124
That’s why my phone died so fast.

358
00:21:49,148 --> 00:21:52,548
That’s rough.

359
00:21:52,571 --> 00:21:54,371
Oh yeah.

360
00:21:54,394 --> 00:21:59,762
My phone battery dropped to 1% at lunchtime.

361
00:21:59,786 --> 00:22:02,519
Just 1%?

362
00:22:02,542 --> 00:22:05,675
That was really risky!

363
00:22:05,698 --> 00:22:07,265
Oh yeah.

364
00:22:07,288 --> 00:22:13,255
I got so scared because I didn’t know the way back to the hotel.

365
00:22:13,278 --> 00:22:14,972
True.

366
00:22:14,996 --> 00:22:18,495
Yeah, no phone, no help.

367
00:22:18,518 --> 00:22:20,318
Oh yeah.

368
00:22:20,341 --> 00:22:24,574
I wish I’d known this before coming to Japan.

369
00:22:24,597 --> 00:22:28,430
Do you always carry a power bank?

370
00:22:28,453 --> 00:22:30,141
Oh yeah.

371
00:22:30,165 --> 00:22:34,056
Well, sometimes I forget.

372
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:37,778
But there’s a great app for situations like this.

373
00:22:37,801 --> 00:22:40,089
What kind of app?

374
00:22:40,113 --> 00:22:43,851
It’s called Charge Spot.

375
00:22:43,874 --> 00:22:46,007
Charge Spot?

376
00:22:46,030 --> 00:22:47,837
Oh yeah.

377
00:22:47,860 --> 00:22:52,956
You can rent a portable battery anywhere in the city.

378
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:55,314
Where can you rent one?

379
00:22:55,338 --> 00:22:58,943
At convenience stores or train stations.

380
00:22:58,967 --> 00:23:01,211
There are tons of them!

381
00:23:01,234 --> 00:23:03,124
Got it.

382
00:23:03,148 --> 00:23:06,814
Can I rent it anywhere?

383
00:23:06,837 --> 00:23:08,604
Oh yeah.

384
00:23:08,627 --> 00:23:13,027
And you can return it at a different location.

385
00:23:13,050 --> 00:23:17,738
Wait, I can return it somewhere else?

386
00:23:17,761 --> 00:23:19,330
Oh yeah.

387
00:23:19,354 --> 00:23:24,154
Yep—rent in Tokyo, return in Osaka if you want!

388
00:23:24,177 --> 00:23:26,977
That’s super convenient!

389
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:28,851
Oh yeah.

390
00:23:28,875 --> 00:23:32,841
You really saved me there!

391
00:23:32,864 --> 00:23:36,240
Haha, glad it helped!

392
00:23:36,264 --> 00:23:42,347
There are so many spots in Japan, so no worries.

393
00:23:42,370 --> 00:23:44,104
True.

394
00:23:44,127 --> 00:23:47,772
You should download the app just in case.

395
00:23:47,795 --> 00:23:52,062
Make sure to install it before coming to Japan!

396
00:23:52,085 --> 00:23:53,494
Oh yeah.

397
00:23:53,517 --> 00:23:56,851
Thanks for the amazing travel hack!

398
00:23:56,874 --> 00:24:00,091
No problem at all!

399
00:24:00,115 --> 00:24:01,615
Scene 9: Travel Food & Saving Money

400
00:24:01,615 --> 00:24:06,448
Next up—let’s talk about food!

401
00:24:06,471 --> 00:24:08,443
Food?

402
00:24:08,467 --> 00:24:12,442
How were the restaurants in Japan?

403
00:24:12,465 --> 00:24:15,665
Everything was delicious!

404
00:24:15,688 --> 00:24:19,621
But a little pricey.

405
00:24:19,644 --> 00:24:21,678
True.

406
00:24:21,701 --> 00:24:26,501
Eating out every day adds up, right?

407
00:24:26,524 --> 00:24:32,724
Yeah, I spent way too much on food during my trip.

408
00:24:32,747 --> 00:24:35,947
That sounds rough!

409
00:24:35,970 --> 00:24:40,589
But I found a great solution!

410
00:24:40,612 --> 00:24:42,491
Where?

411
00:24:42,515 --> 00:24:46,148
Department store basements and supermarkets!

412
00:24:46,171 --> 00:24:49,771
Oh, the depachika?

413
00:24:49,794 --> 00:24:52,675
The basement floors of department stores, right?

414
00:24:52,699 --> 00:24:57,832
Exactly! They have so much amazing food.

415
00:24:57,855 --> 00:25:03,509
Yeah, bento boxes and all kinds of delicious stuff!

416
00:25:03,532 --> 00:25:08,266
I went around 7 PM.

417
00:25:08,289 --> 00:25:11,706
7 PM?

418
00:25:11,729 --> 00:25:16,263
That’s the best time to go!

419
00:25:16,286 --> 00:25:18,054
Why’s that?

420
00:25:18,077 --> 00:25:22,211
Everything gets super cheap.

421
00:25:22,234 --> 00:25:26,567
Ah, they put on discount stickers!

422
00:25:26,590 --> 00:25:30,441
I saw half-price stickers everywhere!

423
00:25:30,465 --> 00:25:35,598
Half off? That’s a steal!

424
00:25:35,621 --> 00:25:41,258
Yep! Sushi and bento boxes were all half price.

425
00:25:41,281 --> 00:25:44,769
How was the supermarket?

426
00:25:44,793 --> 00:25:48,926
Supermarkets get cheaper at night, too.

427
00:25:48,949 --> 00:25:52,716
It's way cheaper than eating at a restaurant.

428
00:25:52,739 --> 00:25:54,939
True.

429
00:25:54,962 --> 00:25:58,697
And it's so delicious!

430
00:25:58,720 --> 00:26:04,853
Yeah. It's cheap and tasty—absolute heaven!

431
00:26:04,876 --> 00:26:09,255
Heaven? Heh, that's funny.

432
00:26:09,278 --> 00:26:14,445
Yeah. It really saved me when I was low on cash.

433
00:26:14,468 --> 00:26:18,884
It's great when you don’t have a restaurant reservation, too.

434
00:26:18,907 --> 00:26:24,074
Yeah, you can relax and eat in your hotel room.

435
00:26:24,097 --> 00:26:27,664
That sounds like a fun way to travel.

436
00:26:27,687 --> 00:26:33,087
I wish I’d known this before coming to Japan!

437
00:26:33,110 --> 00:26:38,877
Right? The late-night department store basements are a must-try.

438
00:26:38,900 --> 00:26:43,667
Definitely hitting them up at night on my next trip.

439
00:26:43,690 --> 00:26:45,190
Scene 10: Shopping in Akihabara

440
00:26:45,190 --> 00:26:49,923
Next up—shopping!

441
00:26:49,946 --> 00:26:52,348
Shopping?

442
00:26:52,371 --> 00:26:56,338
What did you buy in Japan?

443
00:26:56,361 --> 00:27:04,094
I wanted to get some retro Japanese games and anime merch.

444
00:27:04,117 --> 00:27:08,362
Oh, so you went to Akihabara?

445
00:27:08,385 --> 00:27:12,618
Yep, I went to Akihabara.

446
00:27:12,641 --> 00:27:18,449
There were so many huge, interesting shops!

447
00:27:18,473 --> 00:27:21,406
Did you buy anything?

448
00:27:21,429 --> 00:27:25,762
Not really—I didn’t buy much.

449
00:27:25,785 --> 00:27:27,679
Why’s that?

450
00:27:27,703 --> 00:27:33,436
The famous shops were a bit pricey.

451
00:27:33,459 --> 00:27:37,659
Ah, tourist pricing.

452
00:27:37,682 --> 00:27:42,930
Yeah, there are tons of foreign tourists.

453
00:27:42,954 --> 00:27:44,754
True.

454
00:27:44,777 --> 00:27:49,943
I wish I’d known this before coming to Japan.

455
00:27:49,966 --> 00:27:52,539
What kind of hack?

456
00:27:52,563 --> 00:27:58,163
I went to shops a little farther from the tourist spots.

457
00:27:58,186 --> 00:28:01,519
Shops a little farther away?

458
00:28:01,542 --> 00:28:07,743
Yeah, stores like Book Off and Hard Off.

459
00:28:07,767 --> 00:28:10,900
Ah, secondhand shops.

460
00:28:10,923 --> 00:28:17,056
Yeah, they have great stuff at low prices.

461
00:28:17,079 --> 00:28:19,496
Got it.

462
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:26,814
A game that cost 3,000 yen in Akihabara was only 1,000 yen there!

463
00:28:26,837 --> 00:28:30,708
That’s a steal!

464
00:28:30,732 --> 00:28:34,966
Yeah, I was so happy.

465
00:28:34,989 --> 00:28:39,522
Book Off has locations everywhere, right?

466
00:28:39,545 --> 00:28:45,278
Yeah, you can find them easily with a map app.

467
00:28:45,301 --> 00:28:48,676
Just a short train ride away.

468
00:28:48,699 --> 00:28:55,066
It’s a bit of a trip, but it’s worth it.

469
00:28:55,089 --> 00:28:58,762
Great for buying souvenirs, too.

470
00:28:58,785 --> 00:29:04,385
Yeah, books and CDs are super cheap.

471
00:29:04,408 --> 00:29:06,142
True.

472
00:29:06,165 --> 00:29:11,414
I heard about this hack from my otaku friend in Japan.

473
00:29:11,437 --> 00:29:13,971
Good friend to have!

474
00:29:13,994 --> 00:29:18,394
Yeah, I’m really grateful.

475
00:29:18,417 --> 00:29:23,350
Is that the end of your Japan travel stories?

476
00:29:23,373 --> 00:29:27,312
Yeah, we covered a lot!

477
00:29:27,335 --> 00:29:31,935
Yep, so many great tips.

478
00:29:31,958 --> 00:29:38,425
Now my next trip will be even better!

479
00:29:38,449 --> 00:29:43,182
Definitely—come back to Japan soon!

480
00:29:43,205 --> 00:29:47,104
Oh, I’ll be back for sure.

481
00:29:47,128 --> 00:29:48,628
Scene 11: Enjoying Sushi Like a Local at a Popular Spot

482
00:29:48,628 --> 00:29:54,699
This is one of the most popular sushi restaurants in Japan.

483
00:29:54,722 --> 00:29:59,889
Let's enjoy plenty of delicious sushi today!

484
00:29:59,912 --> 00:30:02,927
What a beautiful restaurant!

485
00:30:02,950 --> 00:30:07,226
It has such a traditional Japanese atmosphere.

486
00:30:07,249 --> 00:30:11,816
I'm so excited right now!

487
00:30:11,839 --> 00:30:15,695
There are so many different kinds on the menu.

488
00:30:15,718 --> 00:30:19,885
Which sushi would you like to try first?

489
00:30:19,908 --> 00:30:25,675
I love Japanese tuna.

490
00:30:25,698 --> 00:30:30,376
Let's start with two pieces of tuna.

491
00:30:30,399 --> 00:30:32,132
Got it.

492
00:30:32,155 --> 00:30:37,322
I'll order the tuna from the chef.

493
00:30:37,345 --> 00:30:39,223
Here it comes!

494
00:30:39,247 --> 00:30:44,786
The color is so vibrant—it looks absolutely delicious.

495
00:30:44,809 --> 00:30:47,380
It's fresh!

496
00:30:47,404 --> 00:30:50,604
It's going to be amazing.

497
00:30:50,627 --> 00:30:54,766
Please, go ahead and try it first.

498
00:30:54,789 --> 00:30:56,524
Thank you.

499
00:30:56,547 --> 00:30:59,587
Oh no!

500
00:30:59,611 --> 00:31:03,453
The rice fell apart!

501
00:31:03,476 --> 00:31:08,409
Ah, you ate it in two bites, didn't you?

502
00:31:08,432 --> 00:31:13,766
It's best to eat sushi in one bite if you can.

503
00:31:13,789 --> 00:31:16,434
Really?

504
00:31:16,457 --> 00:31:19,996
The pieces are a little big.

505
00:31:20,020 --> 00:31:24,586
I had to split it into two bites.

506
00:31:24,609 --> 00:31:27,743
Why is one bite better?

507
00:31:27,766 --> 00:31:35,140
If you eat it in two bites, the rice tends to crumble.

508
00:31:35,163 --> 00:31:37,034
I see.

509
00:31:37,057 --> 00:31:42,524
You're right—it did crumble a bit and was harder to eat.

510
00:31:42,548 --> 00:31:47,599
Sushi chefs craft each piece with precision.

511
00:31:47,622 --> 00:31:51,903
It's designed to be the perfect one-bite size for the best balance.

512
00:31:51,927 --> 00:31:57,215
The chef's attention to detail is incredible!

513
00:31:57,238 --> 00:31:59,513
I had no idea.

514
00:31:59,537 --> 00:32:01,708
Exactly.

515
00:32:01,731 --> 00:32:07,265
You eat the fish and rice together in one bite.

516
00:32:07,288 --> 00:32:10,721
That's when it tastes the best.

517
00:32:10,744 --> 00:32:13,417
That's really good to know.

518
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:18,973
Next, I'll order my favorite—salmon—and give it a try.

519
00:32:18,996 --> 00:32:27,996
When adding soy sauce, just dab a little on the fish, not the rice.

520
00:32:28,019 --> 00:32:32,986
I'll be careful this time and eat it neatly in one bite.

521
00:32:33,009 --> 00:32:35,542
You're doing great!

522
00:32:35,565 --> 00:32:40,650
Eating it in one bite looks so elegant.

523
00:32:40,674 --> 00:32:42,841
It really does!

524
00:32:42,864 --> 00:32:45,230
You're absolutely right.

525
00:32:45,253 --> 00:32:48,287
I ate it all in one bite!

526
00:32:48,310 --> 00:32:51,511
The flavors just explode in your mouth.

527
00:32:51,535 --> 00:32:54,935
It's even more delicious this way.

528
00:32:54,958 --> 00:32:58,191
I'm glad you're enjoying it!

529
00:32:58,214 --> 00:33:03,081
This is the proper way to eat sushi in Japan.

530
00:33:03,104 --> 00:33:08,361
It's tricky to pick up sushi with chopsticks.

531
00:33:08,385 --> 00:33:12,251
Is it okay to use my hands if I can't?

532
00:33:12,274 --> 00:33:13,932
Yes, of course.

533
00:33:13,956 --> 00:33:17,523
It's perfectly fine to eat sushi with your hands.

534
00:33:17,546 --> 00:33:21,818
In fact, many people in Japan eat it that way.

535
00:33:21,841 --> 00:33:25,741
So, it's not considered rude to eat with your hands, right?

536
00:33:25,765 --> 00:33:29,831
That's a relief to hear.

537
00:33:29,854 --> 00:33:33,654
From now on, feel free to eat however you like.

538
00:33:33,677 --> 00:33:37,925
Oh, and try the pickled ginger on the side too!

539
00:33:37,949 --> 00:33:41,820
You mean this thin, pink ginger?

540
00:33:41,843 --> 00:33:45,376
When do you usually eat this?

541
00:33:45,399 --> 00:33:50,199
You eat it before switching to a different type of fish.

542
00:33:50,222 --> 00:33:53,625
It refreshes your palate.

543
00:33:53,649 --> 00:33:57,106
That way, the next fish tastes even better.

544
00:33:57,129 --> 00:34:00,536
What a brilliant system!

545
00:34:00,560 --> 00:34:04,326
Now I really understand the rules and etiquette of eating sushi.

546
00:34:04,349 --> 00:34:09,549
Japanese food culture has so many fascinating customs.

547
00:34:09,572 --> 00:34:12,643
I hope you get to experience a lot more.

548
00:34:12,667 --> 00:34:14,200
Oh yeah.

549
00:34:14,223 --> 00:34:20,956
Thank you so much for teaching me how to enjoy sushi today. I really appreciate it!

550
00:34:20,979 --> 00:34:22,479
Scene 12: Struggles with Life in Japan and Japanese Etiquette

551
00:34:22,479 --> 00:34:26,546
My life in Japan has just begun.

552
00:34:26,569 --> 00:34:30,102
How's your daily Japanese study going?

553
00:34:30,125 --> 00:34:33,404
The classes are really interesting.

554
00:34:33,428 --> 00:34:37,661
But there's so much to memorize.

555
00:34:37,684 --> 00:34:40,817
It's still a bit challenging.

556
00:34:40,840 --> 00:34:43,494
It's a whole new environment for you.

557
00:34:43,518 --> 00:34:45,815
Must be tough, huh?

558
00:34:45,839 --> 00:34:50,206
Is there anything else you're struggling with?

559
00:34:50,229 --> 00:34:57,598
Actually, there's something about Japanese manners that I find a bit puzzling.

560
00:34:57,622 --> 00:35:00,057
Manners?

561
00:35:00,080 --> 00:35:04,448
What kind of things are you having trouble with?

562
00:35:04,471 --> 00:35:10,321
Earlier, I greeted my teacher in the hallway.

563
00:35:10,345 --> 00:35:13,878
This was the teacher's reaction.

564
00:35:13,901 --> 00:35:18,301
Oh, I saw it from behind.

565
00:35:18,324 --> 00:35:21,924
Did you notice something odd?

566
00:35:21,947 --> 00:35:25,747
I said, 'Good morning.'

567
00:35:25,770 --> 00:35:27,211
And I put my hands together and bowed.

568
00:35:27,234 --> 00:35:31,434
I neatly placed my hands in front of my chest.

569
00:35:31,457 --> 00:35:33,124
True.

570
00:35:33,147 --> 00:35:36,514
And I bowed deeply.

571
00:35:36,537 --> 00:35:40,104
You sure did bow deeply.

572
00:35:40,127 --> 00:35:45,098
But the teacher looked a bit surprised.

573
00:35:45,121 --> 00:35:48,800
Did I do something wrong?

574
00:35:48,824 --> 00:35:50,359
Oh yeah.

575
00:35:50,382 --> 00:35:57,016
Actually, in Japan, you don't need to put your hands together for a regular greeting.

576
00:35:57,039 --> 00:36:00,105
Really? I didn't know that.

577
00:36:00,128 --> 00:36:04,595
I thought putting my hands together made it more polite.

578
00:36:04,618 --> 00:36:09,856
In my country, we usually hug or shake hands.

579
00:36:09,880 --> 00:36:13,219
Japan is the land of bowing, isn't it?

580
00:36:13,242 --> 00:36:18,442
That's why I thought putting my hands together was the right way to do it.

581
00:36:18,465 --> 00:36:20,199
I see.

582
00:36:20,222 --> 00:36:23,642
I've seen it in anime and movies too.

583
00:36:23,665 --> 00:36:29,879
Foreigners in Japan often put their hands together when they bow, don't they?

584
00:36:29,902 --> 00:36:31,536
Oh yeah.

585
00:36:31,559 --> 00:36:36,963
I thought everyone in Japan put their hands together when bowing.

586
00:36:36,986 --> 00:36:39,920
I had the wrong idea.

587
00:36:39,943 --> 00:36:42,241
Ah, I see.

588
00:36:42,265 --> 00:36:48,031
If you're from a culture where putting your hands together is common, it's not a problem.

589
00:36:48,054 --> 00:36:52,388
But for those from cultures where it's not, it might feel a bit different.

590
00:36:52,411 --> 00:36:58,011
In Japan, when bowing, it's perfectly fine to keep your hands down by your sides.

591
00:36:58,033 --> 00:37:05,481
You're right—none of the Japanese people around here are putting their hands together.

592
00:37:05,505 --> 00:37:07,139
Exactly.

593
00:37:07,162 --> 00:37:09,962
Putting your hands together in Japan.

594
00:37:09,985 --> 00:37:13,273
It's only for special occasions.

595
00:37:13,296 --> 00:37:17,944
What kind of situations would those be, for example?

596
00:37:17,968 --> 00:37:24,236
The most common time you'll see it is before a meal, when saying 'Itadakimasu.'

597
00:37:24,259 --> 00:37:30,393
Ah, right—everyone does put their hands together before eating.

598
00:37:30,416 --> 00:37:32,816
Are there any other times?

599
00:37:32,839 --> 00:37:40,132
Also, when visiting a shrine or temple and praying in front of the gods or Buddha.

600
00:37:40,155 --> 00:37:43,044
Praying to the gods, you mean?

601
00:37:43,067 --> 00:37:46,119
That makes perfect sense.

602
00:37:46,142 --> 00:37:49,276
We use it in everyday conversation too.

603
00:37:49,299 --> 00:37:54,665
Like when you're really begging someone—'Please, I'm counting on you!'

604
00:37:54,688 --> 00:37:58,022
When asking a favor from a friend, for example.

605
00:37:58,045 --> 00:38:00,618
What about when apologizing?

606
00:38:00,641 --> 00:38:05,456
Like when you're in a hurry and say, 'I'm so sorry!'

607
00:38:05,480 --> 00:38:10,053
People sometimes put their hands together then too.

608
00:38:10,076 --> 00:38:14,676
So you don’t put your hands together for a regular morning greeting?

609
00:38:14,699 --> 00:38:16,299
No, you don’t.

610
00:38:16,322 --> 00:38:22,489
For a normal greeting, men usually keep their hands straight by their sides.

611
00:38:22,512 --> 00:38:24,650
I see.

612
00:38:24,673 --> 00:38:29,240
How can women make their bows look graceful?

613
00:38:29,263 --> 00:38:35,136
Women often gently clasp their hands in front of them.

614
00:38:35,160 --> 00:38:38,330
This was really informative!

615
00:38:38,353 --> 00:38:42,401
I’ll practice the proper way to bow starting tomorrow.

616
00:38:42,425 --> 00:38:45,825
Everyone understands you don’t mean any harm.

617
00:38:45,848 --> 00:38:48,921
Take your time getting used to it.

618
00:38:48,945 --> 00:38:51,978
Thank you for your kind words.

619
00:38:52,001 --> 00:38:56,040
I’m glad I’m learning about the real culture of Japan.

620
00:38:56,063 --> 00:38:57,563
Scene 13: The Line at the Popular Ramen Shop

621
00:38:57,563 --> 00:39:04,697
This ramen shop always has a long line outside, doesn’t it?

622
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:06,686
How's your daily Japanese study going?

623
00:39:06,709 --> 00:39:11,076
The delicious aroma is even reaching out here!

624
00:39:11,099 --> 00:39:13,984
It’s definitely worth the wait.

625
00:39:14,008 --> 00:39:16,466
A seat just opened up!

626
00:39:16,489 --> 00:39:19,689
Let’s go in and order.

627
00:39:19,712 --> 00:39:26,346
I’ll go with the warm soy sauce ramen at the top of the menu.

628
00:39:26,369 --> 00:39:31,335
Is there anything else you're struggling with?

629
00:39:31,358 --> 00:39:34,293
Oh, here comes the ramen!

630
00:39:34,317 --> 00:39:37,117
The bowl is hot!

631
00:39:37,140 --> 00:39:42,307
What kind of things are you having trouble with?

632
00:39:42,330 --> 00:39:45,747
Go ahead, eat it while it’s still warm.

633
00:39:45,770 --> 00:39:50,336
But everyone’s eating so quietly.

634
00:39:50,359 --> 00:39:51,854
Oh yeah.

635
00:39:51,878 --> 00:39:57,351
Did you notice something odd?

636
00:39:57,374 --> 00:40:00,308
That would be considered really bad manners.

637
00:40:00,331 --> 00:40:02,767
And I put my hands together and bowed.

638
00:40:02,791 --> 00:40:10,065
But in Japan, it’s actually okay to slurp noodles like ramen.

639
00:40:10,088 --> 00:40:13,298
Wait, really? Is that okay?

640
00:40:13,321 --> 00:40:17,255
Doesn’t it bother the people around you?

641
00:40:17,278 --> 00:40:20,346
No, it doesn’t bother anyone.

642
00:40:20,369 --> 00:40:25,936
In fact, slurping is said to make the food taste even better!

643
00:40:25,959 --> 00:40:27,816
That’s so interesting!

644
00:40:27,839 --> 00:40:32,486
Why does making noise make it taste better?

645
00:40:32,509 --> 00:40:37,943
You draw in air along with the noodles.

646
00:40:37,966 --> 00:40:41,983
That way, you can really savor the amazing aroma of the broth.

647
00:40:42,007 --> 00:40:43,699
I see.

648
00:40:43,722 --> 00:40:49,755
By eating it with air, the aroma becomes even stronger.

649
00:40:49,778 --> 00:40:51,781
Exactly.

650
00:40:51,804 --> 00:40:56,497
Plus, the hot noodles cool down a bit with the air.

651
00:40:56,520 --> 00:41:00,120
That way, you're less likely to burn your mouth.

652
00:41:00,143 --> 00:41:02,977
That’s really great!

653
00:41:03,000 --> 00:41:06,179
My ramen was always too hot.

654
00:41:06,202 --> 00:41:09,014
It was so hard to eat.

655
00:41:09,037 --> 00:41:13,046
Look at the other customers around us.

656
00:41:13,070 --> 00:41:16,480
Everyone’s slurping loudly.

657
00:41:16,504 --> 00:41:18,379
You're absolutely right.

658
00:41:18,402 --> 00:41:25,341
You can hear loud slurping from all over—*slurp, slurp*!

659
00:41:25,364 --> 00:41:30,531
So don’t feel embarrassed here—it’s totally fine!

660
00:41:30,554 --> 00:41:32,287
Understood.

661
00:41:32,310 --> 00:41:35,585
I want to experience Japanese culture.

662
00:41:35,609 --> 00:41:38,609
I’ll give it my best shot!

663
00:41:38,631 --> 00:41:40,965
How is it?

664
00:41:40,988 --> 00:41:45,921
It’s a little tricky at first, but did you manage to slurp well?

665
00:41:45,944 --> 00:41:48,188
What kind of situations would those be, for example?

666
00:41:48,212 --> 00:41:53,485
But the soup tastes even more delicious now than before.

667
00:41:53,508 --> 00:41:55,641
That’s wonderful!

668
00:41:55,664 --> 00:42:01,342
Are there any other times?

669
00:42:01,366 --> 00:42:05,632
The culture of slurping noodles is so fascinating.

670
00:42:05,655 --> 00:42:09,922
Oh, what about when drinking the soup?

671
00:42:09,946 --> 00:42:12,912
Great question!

672
00:42:12,935 --> 00:42:18,702
When drinking the soup, hold the bowl with both hands and sip quietly.

673
00:42:18,725 --> 00:42:23,813
So no loud slurping when drinking the soup, right?

674
00:42:23,837 --> 00:42:26,604
Exactly.

675
00:42:26,627 --> 00:42:31,427
What about when apologizing?

676
00:42:31,449 --> 00:42:33,983
It’s the same ramen,

677
00:42:34,006 --> 00:42:37,772
but the manners are different.

678
00:42:37,795 --> 00:42:40,562
It’s really profound.

679
00:42:40,585 --> 00:42:42,084
True.

680
00:42:42,107 --> 00:42:46,079
Oh, you finished all the soup too!

681
00:42:46,102 --> 00:42:49,547
Yes, it was so delicious.

682
00:42:49,571 --> 00:42:52,348
How can women make their bows look graceful?

683
00:42:52,371 --> 00:42:54,938
It makes it more fun that way.

684
00:42:54,961 --> 00:42:57,232
I’ve grown to love it.

685
00:42:57,255 --> 00:43:00,136
I’m glad you enjoyed it!

686
00:43:00,159 --> 00:43:04,926
Everyone understands you don’t mean any harm.

687
00:43:04,949 --> 00:43:07,982
Absolutely, let’s go again!

688
00:43:08,005 --> 00:43:11,845
Next time, I’ll slurp even better!

689
00:43:11,869 --> 00:43:13,369
Scene 14: Shoe Removal Lesson / How to Take Off Your Shoes at the Entrance

690
00:43:13,369 --> 00:43:18,336
Thank you for coming to my home today.

691
00:43:18,359 --> 00:43:21,216
Please, come in.

692
00:43:21,239 --> 00:43:23,506
Thank you for having me.

693
00:43:23,529 --> 00:43:27,778
This is my first time entering a Japanese home.

694
00:43:27,801 --> 00:43:30,934
I’m really nervous.

695
00:43:30,957 --> 00:43:33,890
Oh, wait a moment.

696
00:43:33,913 --> 00:43:38,113
The way you take off your shoes is a little different.

697
00:43:38,136 --> 00:43:39,750
Huh?

698
00:43:39,774 --> 00:43:44,807
Did I take them off in the wrong spot?

699
00:43:44,830 --> 00:43:47,597
This is the entrance, right?

700
00:43:47,620 --> 00:43:51,553
Yes, this is the right place,

701
00:43:51,576 --> 00:43:55,782
but the direction you face when taking them off is different.

702
00:43:55,806 --> 00:43:58,039
The direction?

703
00:43:58,062 --> 00:44:02,029
I was facing backward.

704
00:44:02,052 --> 00:44:05,926
I was turning my back to the house while taking off my shoes.

705
00:44:05,949 --> 00:44:07,619
True.

706
00:44:07,643 --> 00:44:14,695
In Japan, you shouldn’t turn your back to the house when removing your shoes.

707
00:44:14,719 --> 00:44:19,156
Why can’t you take them off while facing backward?

708
00:44:19,179 --> 00:44:22,146
That’s the easier way.

709
00:44:22,169 --> 00:44:28,569
It’s not good manners to turn your back on people inside the house.

710
00:44:28,592 --> 00:44:30,404
I see.

711
00:44:30,427 --> 00:44:35,605
It’s like turning your back when greeting someone.

712
00:44:35,629 --> 00:44:38,766
That would be rude.

713
00:44:38,790 --> 00:44:41,233
Exactly.

714
00:44:41,256 --> 00:44:47,070
So, first, keep facing forward as you take off your shoes.

715
00:44:47,094 --> 00:44:51,465
Then, step up just like that.

716
00:44:51,488 --> 00:44:53,565
Got it.

717
00:44:53,589 --> 00:44:57,832
Take off your shoes while facing forward.

718
00:44:57,856 --> 00:45:00,534
I’ll place my foot here.

719
00:45:00,557 --> 00:45:03,391
Nice, you’re doing great!

720
00:45:03,414 --> 00:45:09,780
After that, turn around and neatly arrange your shoes.

721
00:45:09,803 --> 00:45:14,470
Turn back and pick up your shoes with your hands.

722
00:45:14,493 --> 00:45:17,426
You turn them outward, right?

723
00:45:17,449 --> 00:45:20,516
Exactly.

724
00:45:20,539 --> 00:45:25,872
Point the toes of your shoes toward the front door.

725
00:45:25,895 --> 00:45:28,751
Toward the door, got it.

726
00:45:28,775 --> 00:45:30,842
Is this okay?

727
00:45:30,865 --> 00:45:34,598
Yes, perfect!

728
00:45:34,621 --> 00:45:41,354
Next time you leave, you can put your shoes on right away.

729
00:45:41,377 --> 00:45:44,111
It’s so convenient!

730
00:45:44,134 --> 00:45:49,467
Definitely—this makes it much easier to put them on next time.

731
00:45:49,490 --> 00:45:55,291
Japanese entryways have so many little customs like this.

732
00:45:55,314 --> 00:45:58,514
It’s really fascinating!

733
00:45:58,537 --> 00:46:02,271
Is it okay to place my shoes in the middle?

734
00:46:02,294 --> 00:46:09,788
No, the middle is where family or other guests pass through.

735
00:46:09,811 --> 00:46:12,545
Place them off to the side.

736
00:46:12,568 --> 00:46:14,501
Understood.

737
00:46:14,524 --> 00:46:19,895
Then I’ll move my shoes to the right side.

738
00:46:19,919 --> 00:46:21,564
Thank you.

739
00:46:21,587 --> 00:46:26,721
You arranged them so neatly—very natural!

740
00:46:26,744 --> 00:46:29,744
There’s a lot to remember.

741
00:46:29,767 --> 00:46:34,300
But Japanese manners make so much sense!

742
00:46:34,323 --> 00:46:38,723
I’m glad you think so!

743
00:46:38,746 --> 00:46:42,014
Now, please come into the living room.

744
00:46:42,038 --> 00:46:44,721
Thank you, excuse me.

745
00:46:44,744 --> 00:46:47,944
I’d love some warm tea.

746
00:46:47,967 --> 00:46:51,767
Of course, I’ll prepare it right away.

747
00:46:51,790 --> 00:46:54,604
Please relax and make yourself at home.

748
00:46:54,628 --> 00:46:56,275
Thank you.

749
00:46:56,298 --> 00:47:00,588
Japanese homes feel so clean and comforting.

750
00:47:00,612 --> 00:47:04,887
Enjoy your time here!

751
00:47:04,910 --> 00:47:08,087
Let’s have a great chat today.

752
00:47:08,110 --> 00:47:11,277
Yes, I’m looking forward to it!

753
00:47:11,300 --> 00:47:16,742
Oh, you can place your bags on the sofa over there.

754
00:47:16,766 --> 00:47:18,454
Thank you.

755
00:47:18,478 --> 00:47:21,447
Where should I hang my coat?

756
00:47:21,470 --> 00:47:24,803
I’ll take care of your coat for you.

757
00:47:24,826 --> 00:47:28,114
I’ll hang it on the coat rack by the entrance.

758
00:47:28,138 --> 00:47:29,638
Scene 15: Conversations at an Izakaya

759
00:47:29,638 --> 00:47:33,371
Great job today—you must be tired!

760
00:47:33,394 --> 00:47:37,603
This izakaya is affordable and delicious!

761
00:47:37,627 --> 00:47:39,827
Thanks for your hard work!

762
00:47:39,850 --> 00:47:44,683
I’ve always wanted to visit a Japanese izakaya!

763
00:47:44,706 --> 00:47:48,473
Let’s order drinks first.

764
00:47:48,496 --> 00:47:51,562
I’ll have a beer.

765
00:47:51,585 --> 00:47:54,957
I’ll go with oolong tea.

766
00:47:54,981 --> 00:47:58,451
I'm really hungry.

767
00:47:58,474 --> 00:48:00,155
Excuse me.

768
00:48:00,179 --> 00:48:03,379
Can we get a beer and some oolong tea, please?

769
00:48:03,402 --> 00:48:10,048
Oh, the server is bringing over some food on a small plate.

770
00:48:10,071 --> 00:48:13,317
Ah, that’s *otōshi*—a little something from the house.

771
00:48:13,340 --> 00:48:17,589
Today’s dish looks like edamame.

772
00:48:17,613 --> 00:48:20,891
Wait, *otōshi*? I didn’t order this.

773
00:48:20,914 --> 00:48:25,081
I didn’t ask for this, though.

774
00:48:25,104 --> 00:48:27,584
I know, I know.

775
00:48:27,607 --> 00:48:33,384
But this is a special system in Japanese izakayas.

776
00:48:33,407 --> 00:48:38,184
It’s strange—food just shows up even though you didn’t order it.

777
00:48:38,207 --> 00:48:41,977
Isn’t this just a mistake by the server?

778
00:48:42,001 --> 00:48:45,568
No, it’s not a mistake.

779
00:48:45,591 --> 00:48:50,682
*Otōshi* is like a seating charge for the restaurant.

780
00:48:50,706 --> 00:48:53,143
A seating charge?

781
00:48:53,167 --> 00:48:57,700
So, it’s like a table charge at restaurants abroad?

782
00:48:57,723 --> 00:48:59,693
Exactly.

783
00:48:59,717 --> 00:49:04,931
Some restaurants overseas do have seating fees, right?

784
00:49:04,955 --> 00:49:07,322
Yeah, they do.

785
00:49:07,345 --> 00:49:12,545
But I’ve never seen one that comes with food before.

786
00:49:12,568 --> 00:49:20,801
In Japan, instead of just charging a seating fee, many places serve this small dish.

787
00:49:20,824 --> 00:49:22,160
I see.

788
00:49:22,184 --> 00:49:28,000
Getting food instead of just paying money is way nicer, don’t you think?

789
00:49:28,023 --> 00:49:29,826
True.

790
00:49:29,849 --> 00:49:35,983
You can snack on this while waiting for your first drink to arrive.

791
00:49:36,006 --> 00:49:39,212
That’s actually a great idea.

792
00:49:39,235 --> 00:49:42,929
This edamame looks delicious.

793
00:49:42,953 --> 00:49:44,286
It pairs really well with alcohol or drinks.

794
00:49:44,309 --> 00:49:48,201
It goes perfectly with drinks.

795
00:49:48,224 --> 00:49:50,829
Go ahead, give it a try.

796
00:49:50,853 --> 00:49:55,253
It’s perfectly salted—so good!

797
00:49:55,276 --> 00:49:58,046
Can we get seconds?

798
00:49:58,069 --> 00:50:02,606
Refills for *otōshi* aren’t usually an option.

799
00:50:02,630 --> 00:50:06,497
But you can order more from the menu.

800
00:50:06,520 --> 00:50:08,388
Understood.

801
00:50:08,411 --> 00:50:13,583
Do all Japanese restaurants have this system?

802
00:50:13,607 --> 00:50:19,140
No, it’s not common in fast food or regular restaurants.

803
00:50:19,163 --> 00:50:23,530
It’s a special culture unique to izakayas.

804
00:50:23,553 --> 00:50:26,386
This was really informative!

805
00:50:26,409 --> 00:50:32,748
When you pay, the *otōshi* charge will be listed on the receipt.

806
00:50:32,772 --> 00:50:35,740
How much does it usually cost?

807
00:50:35,763 --> 00:50:37,737
Is it expensive?

808
00:50:37,760 --> 00:50:40,657
It varies by restaurant.

809
00:50:40,680 --> 00:50:46,118
But it’s usually around 300 to 500 yen.

810
00:50:46,142 --> 00:50:48,742
That’s a relief.

811
00:50:48,765 --> 00:50:52,013
Good to know it’s not too pricey.

812
00:50:52,036 --> 00:50:57,970
Some places even let you decline it if you don’t want it.

813
00:50:57,993 --> 00:50:59,359
Wait, really?

814
00:50:59,382 --> 00:51:02,256
You can refuse it?

815
00:51:02,280 --> 00:51:03,925
Oh yeah.

816
00:51:03,948 --> 00:51:09,122
But explaining that to foreigners can be tricky.

817
00:51:09,146 --> 00:51:12,546
It’s easier to just accept it.

818
00:51:12,569 --> 00:51:17,185
True.

819
00:51:17,208 --> 00:51:20,059
Might as well enjoy it!

820
00:51:20,082 --> 00:51:21,715
Oh, our beer and oolong tea are here!

821
00:51:21,738 --> 00:51:26,505
Let’s cheers to Japanese izakaya culture!

822
00:51:26,528 --> 00:51:31,968
Cheers!

823
00:51:31,992 --> 00:51:33,764
Let's order lots of delicious food!

824
00:51:33,787 --> 00:51:38,400
Oh yeah.

825
00:51:38,424 --> 00:51:39,722
Oh yeah.

826
00:51:39,745 --> 00:51:43,379
I can't wait to look at the menu.

827
00:51:43,402 --> 00:51:44,902
Scene 16: Cultural Differences in Paying the Bill

828
00:51:44,902 --> 00:51:49,180
Wow, I'm so full!

829
00:51:49,204 --> 00:51:53,770
The hamburger steak here was absolutely delicious.

830
00:51:53,793 --> 00:51:57,193
Yes, it was really tasty.

831
00:51:57,216 --> 00:52:00,715
The meat was so tender—it was perfect.

832
00:52:00,739 --> 00:52:03,805
I'm glad you enjoyed it!

833
00:52:03,828 --> 00:52:07,628
Shall we head out soon?

834
00:52:07,651 --> 00:52:09,340
Understood.

835
00:52:09,364 --> 00:52:13,497
Let's call the server and ask for the bill.

836
00:52:13,520 --> 00:52:16,654
Oh, wait a moment.

837
00:52:16,677 --> 00:52:20,532
Don’t take your wallet out on the table.

838
00:52:20,555 --> 00:52:23,407
Wait, why not?

839
00:52:23,430 --> 00:52:28,521
After eating, we pay at the table, right?

840
00:52:28,545 --> 00:52:33,635
In my country, we hand the money to the server at the table.

841
00:52:33,658 --> 00:52:37,102
But in Japan, it's different?

842
00:52:37,126 --> 00:52:42,893
Yes, in most Japanese restaurants, you don’t pay at the table.

843
00:52:42,916 --> 00:52:46,649
So where do we pay?

844
00:52:46,672 --> 00:52:51,742
Look at that small piece of paper on the table.

845
00:52:51,766 --> 00:52:55,619
You mean this paper with all the numbers on it?

846
00:52:55,642 --> 00:52:57,937
What is this?

847
00:52:57,961 --> 00:53:01,219
That’s called the *check* (denpyō).

848
00:53:01,243 --> 00:53:05,813
It lists the dishes we ordered and their prices.

849
00:53:05,837 --> 00:53:07,507
I see.

850
00:53:07,530 --> 00:53:11,578
Where do we go with this check?

851
00:53:11,601 --> 00:53:17,237
Take it to the register near the entrance.

852
00:53:17,261 --> 00:53:20,594
So we pay at the register by the entrance?

853
00:53:20,617 --> 00:53:23,752
We don’t need to call the server?

854
00:53:23,775 --> 00:53:27,790
No, you don’t have to.

855
00:53:27,814 --> 00:53:33,249
Just bring the check to the register, and the staff will handle it.

856
00:53:33,272 --> 00:53:35,239
I see!

857
00:53:35,262 --> 00:53:39,907
It’s so different from my country—I’m a little surprised!

858
00:53:39,931 --> 00:53:45,864
In upscale restaurants or some izakayas, you *can* pay at the table.

859
00:53:45,887 --> 00:53:50,087
So in fancy places, you pay at the table?

860
00:53:50,110 --> 00:53:53,078
Is there a way to tell?

861
00:53:53,102 --> 00:53:58,535
When you’re done eating, the server will place the check on your table.

862
00:53:58,558 --> 00:54:01,691
That’s your cue to take it to the register.

863
00:54:01,714 --> 00:54:03,124
I see.

864
00:54:03,148 --> 00:54:07,392
So if the check is on the table, we bring it to the register ourselves?

865
00:54:07,415 --> 00:54:09,215
Exactly.

866
00:54:09,238 --> 00:54:13,804
If there’s no check, call the server.

867
00:54:13,827 --> 00:54:17,761
Just say, *‘Okaikei o onegaishimasu’* (Check, please).

868
00:54:17,784 --> 00:54:19,717
Understood.

869
00:54:19,740 --> 00:54:24,307
This restaurant has a check, so let’s head to the register.

870
00:54:24,330 --> 00:54:25,748
Oh yeah.

871
00:54:25,772 --> 00:54:32,464
Oh, and when paying, don’t hand the money directly to the staff.

872
00:54:32,487 --> 00:54:33,654
Wait, really?

873
00:54:33,677 --> 00:54:35,713
We can’t hand it to them?

874
00:54:35,736 --> 00:54:39,170
Then where should we put it?

875
00:54:39,193 --> 00:54:42,967
There’s a small tray in front of the register, right?

876
00:54:42,990 --> 00:54:45,608
Place it there.

877
00:54:45,631 --> 00:54:48,831
You mean that blue plastic tray?

878
00:54:48,854 --> 00:54:51,492
Why do we put it on the tray?

879
00:54:51,516 --> 00:54:55,049
To avoid dropping the money.

880
00:54:55,072 --> 00:54:59,405
And it makes it easier for the staff to count.

881
00:54:59,428 --> 00:55:03,395
There are rules for even the smallest details, huh?

882
00:55:03,418 --> 00:55:05,594
I think it’s very efficient!

883
00:55:05,618 --> 00:55:10,184
When paying with a credit card, place it on the tray as well.

884
00:55:10,207 --> 00:55:12,374
Even with a card?

885
00:55:12,397 --> 00:55:15,997
Japanese checkout counters are really unique, aren’t they?

886
00:55:16,020 --> 00:55:19,458
Once you get used to it, it’s very smooth and convenient.

887
00:55:19,482 --> 00:55:22,260
Alright, let’s head to the register.

888
00:55:22,283 --> 00:55:23,616
Oh yeah.

889
00:55:23,639 --> 00:55:27,806
I’ll treat you today, so could you pass me the bill?

890
00:55:27,829 --> 00:55:30,595
Wait, really? Is that okay?

891
00:55:30,618 --> 00:55:33,291
Thank you.

892
00:55:33,315 --> 00:55:35,115
Thank you for the meal!

893
00:55:35,138 --> 00:55:38,383
Oh, you’re welcome!

894
00:55:38,407 --> 00:55:41,655
Let’s go eat something delicious together again soon.

895
00:55:41,678 --> 00:55:42,711
Oh yeah.

896
00:55:42,734 --> 00:55:46,001
Next time, it’s my treat!

897
00:55:46,024 --> 00:55:47,524
Scene 17: Elevator Etiquette

898
00:55:47,524 --> 00:55:50,724
The elevator’s here.

899
00:55:50,747 --> 00:55:53,814
Let’s step inside together.

900
00:55:53,838 --> 00:55:55,652
Thank you.

901
00:55:55,675 --> 00:55:59,769
It’s a bit crowded in here, huh?

902
00:55:59,792 --> 00:56:01,792
True.

903
00:56:01,815 --> 00:56:05,349
We should move to the back.

904
00:56:05,372 --> 00:56:09,338
I’ll stand near the buttons.

905
00:56:09,361 --> 00:56:12,259
Oh, so *that’s* where you stand?

906
00:56:12,283 --> 00:56:13,939
Oh yeah.

907
00:56:13,962 --> 00:56:16,962
This spot won’t get in anyone’s way.

908
00:56:16,985 --> 00:56:21,633
Ah, we’ve reached our floor.

909
00:56:21,657 --> 00:56:25,623
Don’t get off right away!

910
00:56:25,646 --> 00:56:27,680
Wait, why not?

911
00:56:27,703 --> 00:56:31,469
This is my stop.

912
00:56:31,492 --> 00:56:36,581
The person standing by the buttons has a special role.

913
00:56:36,605 --> 00:56:39,499
A special role?

914
00:56:39,522 --> 00:56:41,899
What is it?

915
00:56:41,923 --> 00:56:47,736
You have to hold the door open until everyone else gets off.

916
00:56:47,759 --> 00:56:52,959
So you keep the button pressed for others?

917
00:56:52,982 --> 00:56:54,876
Exactly.

918
00:56:54,899 --> 00:56:57,466
You help everyone exit smoothly.

919
00:56:57,489 --> 00:56:59,756
I had no idea.

920
00:56:59,779 --> 00:57:04,745
I thought you’d get off as soon as your floor came up.

921
00:57:04,768 --> 00:57:09,702
In Japan, the person by the buttons is the last to leave.

922
00:57:09,725 --> 00:57:13,458
That’s such a thoughtful custom.

923
00:57:13,481 --> 00:57:15,140
No, you don’t.

924
00:57:15,163 --> 00:57:19,296
It’s a rule to make sure everyone has a pleasant ride.

925
00:57:19,319 --> 00:57:22,258
What about the close button?

926
00:57:22,282 --> 00:57:26,115
Press it only after everyone’s inside.

927
00:57:26,138 --> 00:57:28,171
Understood.

928
00:57:28,194 --> 00:57:33,128
For now, I’ll keep holding the open button.

929
00:57:33,151 --> 00:57:34,524
Oh yeah.

930
00:57:34,547 --> 00:57:36,123
Perfect!

931
00:57:36,146 --> 00:57:39,280
Everyone’s gotten off now.

932
00:57:39,303 --> 00:57:42,703
Alright, let’s get off too.

933
00:57:42,726 --> 00:57:44,559
Thanks!

934
00:57:44,582 --> 00:57:46,149
You were a big help.

935
00:57:46,172 --> 00:57:50,544
Next time I’m by the buttons, I’ll do my best!